te values in the `status_code` column.\n\nIf you do not have a unique constraint on `field_1`, `field_2` or both, Alison is probably right. Consider you have a table:\n\n| field_1 | field_2 |\n|---------|---------|\n| A       | B      |\n| A       | 10     |\n\nThe first query would eliminate both rows, the second only one.\n\nAlternatively, if you have `NULL` values in the `status_code` column, you may get different results. `(A or not A)` does not work in SQL if you have `NULL` values in columns.\n\nCase 5:\nTo avoid a full table scan with this basic inner join, you can try to add an index on the foreign key (`fkBlob`) in the `metadata` table and also consider adding an index on the condition column (`start`) in the same table.", 'time': 3.715627908706665}
17:54:47,928 root WARNING Failed to generate rewrites from retrieved rewrite cases: Case 1:
The root cause for such a long runtime is that the planner has rewritten the query embedding the view code in an inefficient way.

To modify the query to give results in acceptable times, you can use Common Table Expressions (CTEs) to force the planner to first compute (materialize) the selection from the views:

```sql
WITH 
    a AS MATERIALIZED  (SELECT name, population, place 
                FROM SH_POINT 
                WHERE place IN ('hamlet', 'city', 'town', 'village') ), 
    b AS MATERIALIZED  (SELECT name, population 
                FROM SH_POLY 
                WHERE boundary='administrative' AND admin_level='8' ) 
    
SELECT a.name, a.population, a.place, b.population 
FROM a, b 
WHERE a.name = b.name;
```

